{0 Completion (wikilink suggestions)}

Suggest note names, headings, and attribute ids as the user types inside
wikilink brackets.

{1 Activation}

The server advertises [completionProvider] with trigger characters
[\[\[] and [#]. Completion is requested via [textDocument/completion].

{1:trigger_context Trigger context}

Completion activates when the cursor is inside an incomplete wikilink:

{ul
  {- [\[\[<cursor>\]\]] — typing the note name}
  {- [\[\[note#<cursor>\]\]] — typing a heading or attribute-id fragment}
  {- [!\[\[<cursor>\]\]] — embed link, same behavior}
}

Detection: scan backwards from the cursor for an unmatched [\[\[] (or
[!\[\[]) on the same line. If found, the text after the opening brackets and the
cursor is the {e prefix}.

{1 Completion modes}

{2:note_name_completion Note name completion}

When the prefix contains no [#], suggest note names from the vault
index.

{ul
  {- Each indexed file yields one completion item.}
  {- [label]: the note's shortest unambiguous name (the same name the
     vault resolver would accept).}
  {- [detail]: the full relative path, e.g. ["folder/note.md"].}
  {- [filterText]: the note name, so the client can narrow as the
     user types.}
  {- [kind]: [CompletionItemKind.File].}
}

The {e shortest unambiguous name} is the file's basename without [.md]
when that basename is unique in the vault; otherwise the full relative
path without [.md].

Items are sorted alphabetically by label.

{2:fragment_completion Fragment completion}

When the prefix contains [#], the text before [#] identifies the target
note (or the current file if empty, as in [\[\[#<cursor>\]\]]). The text
after [#] is the fragment prefix.

Suggestions come from the target file's AST:

{ul
  {- {b Headings}: one item per heading.
     [label] is the heading text, [filterText] is its identifier,
     [kind] is [CompletionItemKind.Reference].
     [insertText] is the identifier (e.g. [Section-One]).}
  {- {b Attribute IDs}: one item per explicit djot attribute id
     ([{#id}]); see {!page-"feature-attribute-anchors"}.
     [label] is [#id], [detail] is ["attribute"], [filterText] and
     [insertText] are the bare id (inserted after the [#] the user
     already typed), [kind] is [CompletionItemKind.Reference].}
}

Headings and attribute ids share one fragment namespace, so both are
offered together.

If the target note cannot be resolved, no fragment suggestions are
returned.

{1:completion_item_shape Completion item shape}

{t
  | Field      | Note name mode           | Fragment mode            |
  |------------|--------------------------|--------------------------|
  | label      | shortest unambiguous name | heading text or [#id]    |
  | detail     | full relative path       | —                        |
  | filterText | note name                | identifier               |
  | textEdit   | note name                | identifier               |
  | kind       | File                     | Reference                |
}

The suggestion is delivered as a {b [textEdit]} whose range covers the prefix
already typed — from the [\[\[] (or the [#]) to the cursor — rather than as an
[insertText] the client places by its own word rules. Those rules split on [/],
so a name that fell back to a relative path would otherwise be pasted on top of
what it was completing. The reasoning is
{!page-"feature-completion-markdown-links".replace_range}, which needs the same
range for the same reason; both modes are served by one implementation.

{1 Edge cases}

{ul
  {- {b Cursor not inside [\[\[...\]\]]}: no completions.}
  {- {b Empty vault}: empty completion list.}
  {- {b Ambiguous note name in fragment mode}: resolve using vault
     resolution rules (same as go-to-definition); suggest fragments
     from the first candidate.}
  {- {b Markdown links}: completion inside [()] is
     {!page-"feature-completion-markdown-links"}, a separate spec served by the
     same handler. Wikilink detection runs first, so a [(] inside
     [\[\[note (draft)\]\]] is part of a note name rather than a destination.}
}
